A component is called a Strongly Connected Component(SCC) only if for every possible pair of vertices (u, v) inside that component, u is reachable from v and v is reachable from u.

In the following directed graph, the SCCs have been marked:



By definition, a component containing a single vertex is always a strongly connected component. For that vertex 3 in the above graph is itself a strongly connected component.



Kosaraju’s Algorithm:

/*

    Algorithm:



    1.) Sort all the nodes according to their finishing time

        The node which will finish at last will come on the top of stack



    2.) Reverse all the edges of the entire graph.

        As, Strongly connected components will remain same at their own, but

        this step will avoid to enter DFS from one Strongly Connected Component to

        another.



    3.) Perform the DFS and count the no. of different DFS calls to get the no. of SCC.



    Note:



    ---The first step is to know, from which node we should start the DFS call.



    ---The second step is to make adjacent SCCs unreachable and to limit the

        DFS traversal in such a way, that in each DFS call, all the nodes of a

        particular SCC get visited.



    ---The third step is to get the numbers of the SCCs. In this step, we can

        also store the nodes of each SCC if we want to do so.

*/



class Solution

{

    public:

    void dfs(int node, vector<int> &vis, vector<int> adj[], stack<int> &st) {

        vis[node] = 1;

        for(auto it : adj[node]) {

            if(!vis[it])

                dfs(it, vis, adj, st);

        }



        st.push(node);

    }



    void dfs3(int node, vector<int> &vis, vector<int> adj[]) {



        vis[node] = 1;

        for(auto it : adj[node]) {

            if(!vis[it])

                dfs3(it, vis, adj);

        }

    }



    int kosaraju(int V, vector<int> adj[])

    {

        // Step 1: Sort all the nodes according to their finishing time

        vector<int> vis(V, 0);

        stack<int> st;

        for(int i = 0; i < V; i++) {

            if(!vis[i])

                dfs(i, vis, adj, st);

        }



        // Step 2: Reverse all the edges of the entire graph.

        //          and mark all nodes as unvisited.

        vector<int> graph[V];

        for(int i=0; i < V; i++) {

            vis[i] = 0;



            // i -> it

            // it -> i

            for(auto it : adj[i]) {

                graph[it].push_back(i);

            }

        }





        // Step 3: Perform the DFS and count the no. of different

        //          DFS calls to get the no. of SCC.

        int scc = 0;

        while(!st.empty()) {

            int node = st.top();

            st.pop();



            if(!vis[node]) {

                scc++;

                dfs3(node, vis, graph);

            }

        }

        return scc;

    }

};

